    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "With the increasing popularity of Web3 applications, pro-\r\ntecting digital secrets in consumer devices is more impor-\r\ntant than ever before. While security sensitive organizations\r\ntypically use an \"air gapped\" strategy where one device is\r\nconnected to a network for primary use and a secondary non-\r\nnetwork connected device is used to store sensitive informa-\r\ntion, this solution is not practical for the common user.\r\n\r\nIn this paper, we introduce a novel \"boot gapped\" system,\r\nwhere a single device can vector between a primary OS and\r\na secure isolated OS through a firmware defined interface.\r\nEach OS runs natively, taking turns controlling the system\r\nwithout a hypervisor or virtual machine, significantly reduc-\r\ning overhead and the attack surface. A collection of Unified\r\nExtensible Firmware Interface (UEFI) drivers provide isola-\r\ntion and facilitate switching from one OS to another. When a\r\nswitch is initiated, the current OS will use the standard Ad-\r\nvanced Configuration and Power Interface (ACPI) suspend\r\nfunctionality to save its state, then signal a UEFI driver. The\r\ndriver securely facilitates the switch while enforcing isolation\r\nand providing a communication buffer between the OSes. Fi-\r\nnally, the driver triggers the standard ACPI resume call in the\r\nother OS, completing the switch.\r\n\r\nThe system is implemented entirely in software and is com-\r\npatible with UEFI x86-64 devices with only minor changes\r\nto standard bootloaders. While this work is ongoing, we hope\r\nto achieve a fast, secure multi-boot system compatible with\r\nmost consumer devices.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
